1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,924
This is a DIY RC sim-racing controller: a
receiver adapter, a handful of firmware, a

2
00:00:05,924 --> 00:00:10,579
custom case I modelled myself, and then a
rather brave track test.

3
00:00:10,929 --> 00:00:16,636
Here it is on track — the RC controller in
one hand while I drive, and that receiver

4
00:00:16,636 --> 00:00:21,323
signal is what my little adapter turns
into an ordinary USB joystick.

5
00:00:21,723 --> 00:00:27,631
Let us look at the firmware, because the
whole trick is only a handful of lines. It

6
00:00:27,631 --> 00:00:30,621
is an Arduino sketch called rc controller.

7
00:00:30,971 --> 00:00:36,969
First it pulls in Joystick dot h, a
library that turns the board into a
standard USB

8
00:00:36,969 --> 00:00:40,610
game controller, so the PC needs no
special driver.

9
00:00:40,910 --> 00:00:46,231
Then it names the two inputs: throttle on
digital pin two, and steering on digital

10
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:46,880
pin three.

11
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:52,800
In setup, both pins are set as inputs, and
Joystick dot begin hands the controller

12
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:54,788
over to the operating system.

13
00:00:55,088 --> 00:01:00,675
The clever part is the axis ranges: set X
axis range from nineteen hundred down to

14
00:01:00,675 --> 00:01:03,809
ten fifty, and the Y axis the other way
round.

15
00:01:04,109 --> 00:01:09,318
An RC receiver sends a pulse between
roughly one and two milliseconds, and the
ends

16
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:14,212
come in reversed — so the firmware
deliberately flips them back into the
right

17
00:01:14,212 --> 00:01:14,840
direction.

18
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:20,210
The main loop then does the real work.
pulse in measures the length of the pulse

19
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:23,938
arriving on each pin, and that length is
the stick position.

20
00:01:24,238 --> 00:01:30,312
Steering goes to the X axis and throttle
to the Y axis, so the transmitter moves
the

21
00:01:30,312 --> 00:01:33,276
joystick. And that is the entire adapter.

22
00:01:33,676 --> 00:01:39,864
The board needed a home, so I modelled a
case in 3table, the CAD editor I built

23
00:01:39,864 --> 00:01:40,412
myself.

24
00:01:40,712 --> 00:01:46,327
Starting from a blank grid, I shaped a
long grey shell around the board —
snap-fit

25
00:01:46,327 --> 00:01:48,587
tabs and a power switch up front.

26
00:01:48,887 --> 00:01:54,556
Inside there are ribs, threaded mounting
posts and slots that hold the board
exactly

27
00:01:54,556 --> 00:01:56,041
where it needs to sit.

28
00:01:56,441 --> 00:02:02,139
Then came the track test. Full disclosure:
I was filming with one hand and steering

29
00:02:02,139 --> 00:02:07,699
with the other, so the driving is,
frankly, brave. But the controller worked,
and

30
00:02:07,699 --> 00:02:09,347
that is the whole point.

31
00:02:09,747 --> 00:02:15,779
A receiver adapter, a few dozen lines of
firmware, a case modelled in 3table, and

32
00:02:15,779 --> 00:02:17,343
one very sketchy lap.

33
00:02:17,693 --> 00:02:19,368
Thanks for watching.
